  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Low pressure moves offshore tonight and slowly \npulls away, resulting in some unsettled weather through the rest\nof the week. \n\nA vigorous upper-level trough continues to evolve across the Eastern\nU.S. for tonight and into the weekend. This supported the \nsurface low deepening near our CWA today. The low then heads NE\nour across the waters off New England Friday. While there are \nstill some differences with the handling of the embedded \nshortwaves, there is better model clustering with the surface \nlow placement/track. Overall, it will be unsettled across our \nregion tonight and Friday.\n\nThe tornado threat is over for today and the Tornado Watch has\nbeen cancelled. Just some scattered showers expected through the\nrest of the night. \n\nNot much has changed with regards to the cold air following the\nsystem and the subsequent change in p-type for NW areas. The \nstrength of the parent upper-level trough results in a colder \nair mass moving in with time, however the initial air mass is \nnot all that cold (the lack of a cold antecedent air mass). As \nthe surface low consolidates off the northern Mid-Atlantic \ncoast, the winds will turn more out of the northwest and this \nwill increase the cold air advection. This will result in some \ncolder air seeping southeastward later tonight but especially \nFriday into Friday night. The air mass may become sufficiently \ncold enough mostly across our far northwestern zones for some \nsnow across the higher elevations, confined mainly to the Pocono\nPlateau. Any snowfall accumulation is expected to be less than \nan inch. Some elevated locations across the Lehigh Valley and \nNorthwestern NJ may see a few wet flakes mixing in, but no \naccumulations are expected. Otherwise, a rain event is forecast.\nThe amount of precipitation (rain and/or snow) will depend on \nthe ultimate track of the surface low and whether a deformation \nzones wraps back into our northern to central areas. At the \nmoment, rainfall amounts north of the PA Turnpike to I-195 \ncorridor range around the 0.50-1.25\" range while further south \nacross Philly and south, we are expecting less than 0.50\" of \nrainfall, with less than 0.10\" expected for the southern \nDelmarva.\n \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A stretch of below normal temperatures begins \nFriday, continuing through the weekend as a much colder airmass \nmoves overhead.\n\nIn the wake of the storm, as it deepens and tracks toward the Canadian\nMaritimes over the weekend, a colder air mass will be in place across\nour area. In addition, a tightened pressure gradient coupled with\ndeeper mixing will result in a period of breezy to windy conditions\nover the weekend. The strongest winds look to occur on Saturday,\nwith peak gusts to about 30 mph, then while still gusty on Sunday\nit appears the speeds come down a little. Daytime temperatures \non Friday and Saturday should not get out of the 40s for many areas,\nwith temperatures at night dropping into the 20s to low 30s. Where\nthe growing season is still active across Delmarva, parts of southern\nNew Jersey and far southeastern Pennsylvania, frost/freeze headlines\nmay be necessary. There could even be some lake effect snow and/or\nrain showers into the southern Poconos on Saturday.\n\nTemperatures start to moderate a bit Sunday through Tuesday. Despite\nthat, high temperatures are only in the upper 40s to low 50s each\nday which is still below average for this time of year.\n\nWhile we are likely to get our first freeze sometime during this\nstretch, the Frost/Freeze program area-wide ends on Sunday November\n15th. Beyond that, no further frost/freeze headlines will be issued.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Widespread minor tidal flooding expected along \nthe New Jersey coast on Friday morning. \n\nStrong easterly flow with a deepening area of low pressure\nresulted in a push of water towards the New Jersey coast. Winds\nwill turn a bit more northerly, though the direction overall\nwill not be west enough to drain the back bays. As a result,\nwidespread minor tidal flooding is expected with tomorrow\nmorning's high tide along the Raritan Bay and immeadiate\ncoastline. A Coastal Flood Advisory was issued. \n\nFlow has turned more westerly around Delaware Bay, southern\nDelaware, and Chesapeake Bay. As a result, only spotty minor\ntidal flooding at most is expected. \n\nNo further tidal issues are anticipated after Friday morning's\nhigh tide.",
